Jym <moyen@loria.fr> writes:

> Why do the patch not put in themsleves the #define for their options ?
> (maybe because then the config.h of a multi-patched version will be a real
> mess an not easily repatched by the following one even if each patch only

This is the main reason.
People who throw one patch in are likely to try other patches as well, and
when multiple patches all change config.h probably around the same place,
the patch utility wouldn't necessarily be able to change it, so people
would need to edit it manually anyway.

We were talking about the sortloot patch, which is located on my page (and
probably on some others as well). And I have written instructions on that
page for each patch, what you need to #define to make them work..

> > #define SORTLOOT in include/config.h before compiling.
> Is there some canonical place to put it in the file ?

At the end of the file there is a header in the comments "Section 5:
EXPERIMENTAL STUFF", and the comment text says "Conditional compilation of
new or experimental options are controlled here". So that's an obvious
place for definitions for patches.

> (AFAIK, the precise place would have no importance (at least as long as
> there is no other #define with the same name), but it would certainly be a
> good idea to keep the file well ordered in oreder to be able to track back

